  <Fragment slot="content">
    <section class="fd-content">
      <h2>FD Calculator – Fixed Deposit Maturity & Interest Calculator</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Fixed Deposit (FD) Calculator</strong> is an essential financial planning tool that helps you calculate the exact maturity amount, total interest earned, and effective yield on your fixed deposit investment. Whether you are investing in SBI, HDFC, ICICI, Axis, or any other bank, our FD calculator instantly computes your returns with quarterly, monthly, or annual compounding options.
      </p>

      <h2>What Is a Fixed Deposit (FD)?</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Fixed Deposit</strong> is one of India's most popular and safest investment instruments offered by banks, post offices, and non-banking financial companies (NBFCs). You deposit a lump sum for a fixed tenure at a predetermined interest rate, and at maturity, you receive the principal plus accumulated interest. FDs are risk-free (DICGC insured up to ₹5 lakhs per depositor per bank), making them ideal for conservative investors seeking guaranteed returns.
      </p>

      <h2>FD Maturity Formula</h2>
      <p>For <strong>compound interest FD</strong> (most banks use quarterly compounding):</p>
      <div class="my-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-100/80 p-4 font-mono text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
        <div>M = P × (1 + r/n)^(n×t)</div>
        <div class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Where: P = Principal | r = Annual Rate | n = Compounding periods per year | t = Time in years</div>
      </div>
      <p>For <strong>simple interest FD</strong>: <code>Interest = P × r × t / 100</code></p>

      <h2>FD Interest Rates in India (2025)</h2>
      <p>Leading bank FD rates for general public (regular FDs, 1-year tenure):</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>SBI:</strong> 6.80% – 7.10% (1–3 years)</li>
        <li><strong>HDFC Bank:</strong> 6.60% – 7.35%</li>
        <li><strong>ICICI Bank:</strong> 6.70% – 7.25%</li>
        <li><strong>Axis Bank:</strong> 6.70% – 7.25%</li>
        <li><strong>Small Finance Banks:</strong> 7.50% – 9.00% (higher risk)</li>
        <li><strong>Senior Citizens:</strong> 0.25%–0.50% extra on all above rates</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Senior Citizen FD Benefits</h2>
      <p>Indian banks offer an additional <strong>0.25% to 0.50% per annum</strong> interest rate to depositors aged 60 years and above. The TDS threshold for senior citizens is also higher at ₹50,000 per year (vs ₹40,000 for regular depositors), meaning less TDS deduction on FD interest income.</p>

      <h2>Tax on FD Interest</h2>
      <p>FD interest is fully taxable as <strong>"Income from Other Sources"</strong> under the Income Tax Act, 1961. Key tax rules:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>TDS Rate:</strong> 10% is deducted if annual interest exceeds ₹40,000 (₹50,000 for senior citizens)</li>
        <li><strong>Tax Slab:</strong> You pay tax as per your income tax bracket (0% to 30%)</li>
        <li><strong>Form 15G/15H:</strong> Submit to avoid TDS if your total income is below the taxable limit</li>
        <li><strong>Tax-Saver FD:</strong> 5-year FD qualifies for ₹1.5 lakh deduction under Section 80C</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Cumulative vs Non-Cumulative FD</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Cumulative FD:</strong> Interest compounds and is paid at maturity. Best for wealth accumulation. Higher effective returns.</li>
        <li><strong>Non-Cumulative FD:</strong> Interest paid monthly, quarterly, or annually. Ideal for regular income (pensioners, retirees).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Tips to Maximize FD Returns</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Laddering Strategy:</strong> Split large deposits into multiple FDs with staggered maturities to maintain liquidity and capture better rates.</li>
        <li><strong>Compare Banks:</strong> Small finance banks and NBFCs offer 1%–2% higher rates, though with slightly more risk.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose Quarterly Compounding:</strong> Always prefer quarterly over annual compounding — it gives a higher effective annual yield.</li>
        <li><strong>Submit 15G/15H:</strong> Submit Form 15G (or 15H for seniors) to your bank to prevent TDS deduction if eligible.</li>
        <li><strong>Joint Account:</strong> Open FD jointly with a family member in a lower tax bracket to reduce the overall tax burden.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>
