    <div class="space-y-4 text-xs sm:text-sm">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800" open>
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">1. How does an FD calculator work?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">An FD calculator uses the compound interest formula M = P × (1 + r/n)^(nt) to calculate maturity amount. You enter the principal, annual interest rate, tenure, and compounding frequency. The calculator then shows you the maturity amount and total interest earned instantly.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">2. What is the FD interest rate in India in 2025?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">In 2025, major bank FD rates range from 6.5% to 7.5% for regular depositors. Small Finance Banks like AU, ESAF, and Jana offer 7.5%–9.0%. Senior citizens get an additional 0.25%–0.50% on top of regular rates.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">3. Is FD interest taxable in India?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Yes, FD interest is fully taxable as "Income from Other Sources." TDS at 10% is deducted if annual interest exceeds ₹40,000 (₹50,000 for senior citizens). You pay additional tax as per your income tax slab when filing ITR.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">4. What is the difference between cumulative and non-cumulative FD?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Cumulative FD reinvests the interest (compounds it), so you receive higher maturity amount. Non-cumulative FD pays interest periodically (monthly/quarterly) — suitable for regular income. Cumulative FD gives better overall returns due to compounding.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">5. What is the minimum and maximum FD investment?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">The minimum FD amount varies by bank — usually ₹1,000 to ₹10,000. There is typically no maximum limit for regular FDs. However, DICGC insurance covers only up to ₹5 lakhs per depositor per bank, so consider spreading large deposits.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">6. Can I break my FD before maturity?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Yes, most banks allow premature FD withdrawal, but they charge a penalty of 0.5%–1% less than the applicable rate. Tax-Saver FDs (5-year, 80C) cannot be broken before 5 years. Some banks offer auto-sweep FDs with zero penalty.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-4 dark:border-slate-800">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none"><h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">7. What is effective yield in FD, and why does it matter?<span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span></h3></summary>
        <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">Effective yield is the actual annual return when compounding is applied. For example, a 7.1% rate compounded quarterly gives an effective yield of approximately 7.27%. The more frequent the compounding, the higher the effective yield — even at the same nominal rate.</p>
      </details>
